import MVar
/-!
# BChan

Bounded channels with backpressure: `import BChan` (stage 25f,
docs/stages/25-concurrency.md).

A `Chan` (lib/Session.wiz) buffers without limit: `send` never blocks, so a producer
that runs faster than its consumer grows the heap. A `BChan A` is a bounded FIFO
queue that any number of threads may share: the sender at the ring position waits
while all `capacity` slots hold unconsumed items (other senders wait behind it), and
`recv` waits while the next slot is empty, so the two sides run at the slower one's
pace. Unlike `Chan`, a `BChan` is an ordinary (ω) value: it can be copied, stored and
passed to both threads, and there is no protocol type to keep in step.

Order: both sides take turns by borrowing one of two `MVar`s (`inPos`, `outPos`)
that hold the ring position, so sends are written to the ring one at a time and
receives read it in the same order. Receives therefore see items in the order they
were sent (which thread receives which item is up to the scheduler).

**Limits.** This is a buffer, not a protocol: there is no `close`, no `trySend` and
no `select`, and dropping a `BChan` does not wake a thread blocked on it. A thread
blocked in `send`/`recv` cannot be cancelled, and the runtime waits for every
thread at exit (25a), so a producer with no consumer hangs the process. A
`capacity` of 0 is treated as 1 (an `MVar` slot always holds at most one item, so
there is no rendezvous mode). Blocking is built from the MVar mutexes and condition
variables, not from atomics: Wiz has no user-level atomics or fences, and the RC
counts are relaxed (their order comes from the MVar locks); do not build lock-free
structures on the RC words.

```
def main : IO Unit := IO.bind (BChan.new (A := Nat) 8) (fun c =>
  IO.bind (BChan.send c 1) (fun _ =>
    IO.bind (BChan.recv c) (fun x => IO.println (Nat.toString x))))
```

Compiled code only: the interpreters (`#eval`, `#run`) don't run the MVar natives.
-/

/-- The ring's position: the next slot, and the slots after it in order. Internal:
`BChan`'s constructor and position fields are private, so the ring can't get out of
step with a user's own `MVar`s. -/
private structure BChan.Ring (A : Type) where
  head : MVar A
  rest : List (MVar A)

/-- Advance the ring by one slot (wrapping around a one-slot ring). -/
private def BChan.Ring.next {A : Type} (r : BChan.Ring A) : BChan.Ring A :=
  match r.rest with
  | .nil => r
  | .cons x xs => BChan.Ring.mk x xs

/-- `n` empty one-place slots, in a ring. `n ≥ 1` (`BChan.new` ensures it). -/
private def BChan.mkRing {A : Type} : Nat → IO (BChan.Ring A)
  | .zero => IO.bind (MVar.new (A := A)) (fun m => IO.pure (BChan.Ring.mk m .nil))
  | .succ k => IO.bind (BChan.mkRing k) (fun r =>
      IO.bind (MVar.new (A := A)) (fun m =>
        IO.pure (BChan.Ring.mk m (.cons r.head r.rest))))

/-- A bounded FIFO channel holding at most `capacity` unconsumed items. -/
structure BChan (A : Type) where
  private mk ::
  /-- the effective capacity (`Nat.max 1 capacity`) -/
  capacity : Nat
  /-- the ring position of the next `send` -/
  private inPos : MVar (BChan.Ring A)
  /-- the ring position of the next `recv` -/
  private outPos : MVar (BChan.Ring A)

/-- A new bounded channel with `capacity` slots (at least one: 0 is treated as 1). -/
def BChan.new {A : Type} (capacity : Nat) : IO (BChan A) :=
  let n := Nat.max 1 capacity;
  IO.bind (BChan.mkRing (A := A) n) (fun r =>
    IO.bind (MVar.newFull (A := BChan.Ring A) r) (fun inPos =>
      IO.bind (MVar.newFull (A := BChan.Ring A) r) (fun outPos =>
        IO.pure (BChan.mk n inPos outPos))))

/-- Put `a` at the end of the channel. Waits while all slots hold unconsumed items.

`inPos` is held across the (possibly blocking) `MVar.put`, so sends reach the ring
in one order even with several senders; the slot's own `MVar` is the condition
variable that waits for the consumer. -/
def BChan.send {A : Type} (c : BChan A) (a : A) : IO Unit :=
  MVar.borrow c.inPos (fun r h =>
    IO.bind (MVar.put r.head a) (fun _ =>
      MVar.fill c.inPos h (BChan.Ring.next r) (IO.pure ())))

/-- Take the oldest item. Waits while the next slot is empty.

`outPos` is held across the (possibly blocking) `MVar.take`, so receives happen in
the ring's order (the order the items were sent). -/
def BChan.recv {A : Type} (c : BChan A) : IO A :=
  MVar.borrow c.outPos (fun r h =>
    IO.bind (MVar.take r.head) (fun a =>
      MVar.fill c.outPos h (BChan.Ring.next r) (IO.pure a)))
